"45a_life.mst — Conway's Game of Life in the native Metal game pane
 (docs/gamepane_design.md). Launch it from the Demos menu, or a Workspace:

     Life launch

 SPACE pauses and resumes, the LEFT mouse button draws live cells, the RIGHT
 one erases them, and Escape quits — and because drawing reads the button's
 held state rather than a click, dragging paints a stroke of cells. Editing
 works while the simulation is running as well as while it is paused; pausing
 just makes it easy to aim.

 This is the demo the game pane's MOUSE support was built for. Keys arrive as
 a bitmask on `stepWithKeys:`; the pointer now rides along with them
 (`stepWithKeys:mouseX:y:buttons:`), already converted by the GUI into PANE
 PIXELS from the top-left — the same coordinates `point:y:color:` and `blit:`
 use — so a demo never sees a window point or has to care that the game window
 is resizable.

 The grid is 80x60 cells of 4x4 pixels, which tiles the 320x240 pane exactly;
 each cell leaves its last row and column as background, and those gutters ARE
 the grid lines (no separate line drawing). A whole frame is composed into one
 reused ByteArray of palette indices and handed over in a single `blit:`
 (primitive 215) — the bulk path, one command per frame instead of 76,800
 `point:y:color:` sends — and is only recomposed when something actually
 changed, so a paused grid costs nothing to hold on screen.

 The world wraps: the grid is a torus, so a glider leaving the right edge
 returns at the left, and no rule needs an edge case. In a headless VM every
 game primitive silently fails, so loading this class outside the GUI is
 harmless — which is exactly how its regression test drives it."
"I am Conway's Game of Life running live in the native game pane: an 80x60
 torus of cells stepped by the classic rules — a live cell with 2 or 3 live
 neighbours survives, a dead cell with exactly 3 is born, everything else
 dies. Launch me from the Demos menu, or a Workspace: Life launch. SPACE
 pauses and resumes me, the LEFT mouse button draws cells and the RIGHT one
 erases (hold and drag to paint a stroke), Escape quits. I advance one
 generation every fourth frame so the evolution is watchable, repaint only
 when something changed, and hand each frame to the pane in a single blit:.
 Harmless to load in a headless VM (every game primitive silently fails
 there)."
Object subclass: Life [
    | pane cols rows cell cells next buf gen running spaceWas ticks perGen
      dirty aliveC deadC gridC |
    <classVars: Current>

    Life class >> launch [
        "Open the game pane and start the simulation running."
        ^self new start
    ]

    Life class >> current [
        "The Life most recently launched — so a Workspace can question the
         simulation while it runs (`Life current population`, `Life current
         at: 10 y: 4`), which is the whole point of a demo you can read in the
         Browser while it plays. Also how the regression test reaches the
         grid."
        ^Current
    ]

    start [
        Current := self.
        pane := GamePane new.
        cell := 4.
        cols := 320 // cell.        "80 cells across"
        rows := 240 // cell.        "60 cells down"
        cells := ByteArray new: cols * rows.
        next := ByteArray new: cols * rows.
        buf := ByteArray new: 320 * 240.
        "Cache the palette indices: paint reads them once per pixel, and a
         message send per pixel is 76,800 sends a frame for numbers that
         never change."
        aliveC := GamePane green.
        deadC := GamePane midnight.
        gridC := GamePane black.
        gen := 0.
        ticks := 0.
        perGen := 4.                "advance every 4th frame — 15 generations/s"
        running := true.
        spaceWas := false.
        dirty := true.
        self seed.
        "Take the frame as a parameter — the engine hands each tick's keys and
         pointer over as one object, so this demo never reads global input
         state and keeps working unchanged when the frame learns a new one."
        pane onStep: [ :frame | self stepWith: frame ]; run.
        ^self
    ]

    seed [
        "Two classic patterns: the R-pentomino, five cells that stay chaotic
         for over a thousand generations before settling, and a glider walking
         away from the corner — enough that the pane is interesting the moment
         it opens, and both are small enough to recognise while they run."
        | cx cy |
        cx := cols // 2.
        cy := rows // 2.
        ".XX
          XX.
          .X."
        self setX: cx y: cy - 1.
        self setX: cx + 1 y: cy - 1.
        self setX: cx - 1 y: cy.
        self setX: cx y: cy.
        self setX: cx y: cy + 1.
        "A glider, heading down and right."
        self setX: 2 y: 1.
        self setX: 3 y: 2.
        self setX: 1 y: 3.
        self setX: 2 y: 3.
        self setX: 3 y: 3.
        ^self
    ]

    "--- the grid ---"

    setX: x y: y [ "Bring the cell at (x, y) to life." ^self at: x y: y put: 1 ]

    at: x y: y put: v [
        "Set the cell at (x, y), ignoring anything off the grid — the mouse can
         report a pixel on the very edge, and a stray click should do nothing
         rather than fail."
        (x >= 0 and: [ x < cols and: [ y >= 0 and: [ y < rows ] ] ])
            ifTrue: [ cells at: (y * cols) + x + 1 put: v ].
        ^self
    ]

    at: x y: y [ "The cell at (x, y): 1 alive, 0 dead." ^cells at: (y * cols) + x + 1 ]

    population [
        "How many cells are alive — the HUD's second number."
        | n |
        n := 0.
        1 to: cells size do: [ :i | n := n + (cells at: i) ].
        ^n
    ]

    generation [ "Generations elapsed since launch." ^gen ]

    isRunning [ "Is the simulation advancing, or paused?" ^running ]

    neighboursAtX: x y: y [
        "The eight neighbours of (x, y), counted on a torus: the grid wraps at
         every edge, so a glider that walks off one side reappears on the other
         and no rule needs a boundary case."
        | n xm xp ym yp |
        xm := (x + cols - 1) \\ cols.
        xp := (x + 1) \\ cols.
        ym := (y + rows - 1) \\ rows.
        yp := (y + 1) \\ rows.
        n := 0.
        n := n + (cells at: (ym * cols) + xm + 1).
        n := n + (cells at: (ym * cols) + x + 1).
        n := n + (cells at: (ym * cols) + xp + 1).
        n := n + (cells at: (y * cols) + xm + 1).
        n := n + (cells at: (y * cols) + xp + 1).
        n := n + (cells at: (yp * cols) + xm + 1).
        n := n + (cells at: (yp * cols) + x + 1).
        n := n + (cells at: (yp * cols) + xp + 1).
        ^n
    ]

    advance [
        "One generation of Conway's rules — a live cell with 2 or 3 live
         neighbours survives, a dead cell with exactly 3 is born, everything
         else is dead next generation. Written into the spare grid and then
         SWAPPED in: computing in place would let a cell that had already been
         updated count as its own neighbour."
        | i n alive t |
        0 to: rows - 1 do: [ :y |
            0 to: cols - 1 do: [ :x |
                i := (y * cols) + x + 1.
                n := self neighboursAtX: x y: y.
                alive := (cells at: i) = 1.
                (alive
                    ifTrue: [ n = 2 or: [ n = 3 ] ]
                    ifFalse: [ n = 3 ])
                        ifTrue: [ next at: i put: 1 ]
                        ifFalse: [ next at: i put: 0 ] ] ].
        "Swap rather than copy: the new grid becomes live, and the old one is
         next generation's scratch."
        t := cells.
        cells := next.
        next := t.
        gen := gen + 1.
        dirty := true.
        ^self
    ]

    "--- input ---"

    readSpaceFrom: frame [
        "SPACE toggles pause. The key mask is LEVEL-triggered — it reports
         'held', not 'pressed' — so the toggle fires only on the frame the key
         goes down; reading the level directly would flip pause 60 times a
         second for as long as the key was held."
        | down |
        down := frame keyHeld: GamePane keyA.
        (down and: [ spaceWas not ]) ifTrue: [ running := running not ].
        spaceWas := down.
        ^self
    ]

    readMouseFrom: frame [
        "Left button draws, right button erases. Held state, not a click, so
         holding a button and dragging paints a stroke — and either works
         whether the simulation is running or paused."
        | x y |
        frame mouseInPane ifFalse: [ ^self ].
        (frame mouseDown or: [ frame mouseRightDown ]) ifFalse: [ ^self ].
        x := frame mouseX // cell.
        y := frame mouseY // cell.
        frame mouseDown
            ifTrue: [ self at: x y: y put: 1 ]
            ifFalse: [ self at: x y: y put: 0 ].
        dirty := true.
        ^self
    ]

    "--- the frame ---"

    stepWith: frame [
        "One frame, given that frame's parameters: read the input, advance the
         simulation if it is running and this tick is a generation tick,
         repaint if anything changed, and present. `present` runs every frame —
         it is the one moment the GUI shows the pane — while the repaint above
         it is skipped whenever the grid is unchanged, which is most frames
         while paused."
        ticks := ticks + 1.
        self readSpaceFrom: frame.
        self readMouseFrom: frame.
        (running and: [ ticks \\ perGen = 0 ]) ifTrue: [ self advance ].
        dirty ifTrue: [ self paint. dirty := false ].
        self drawHud.
        pane present.
        ^self
    ]

    paint [
        "Compose the whole frame into `buf` — one palette index per pane pixel,
         row-major from the top-left — and blit it in a single command. Each
         cell fills a `cell`x`cell` block whose last row and column are left as
         grid colour, so the gutters draw the lattice for free."
        | idx colour base |
        0 to: rows - 1 do: [ :y |
            0 to: cols - 1 do: [ :x |
                colour := (cells at: (y * cols) + x + 1) = 1
                    ifTrue: [ aliveC ]
                    ifFalse: [ deadC ].
                base := (y * cell * 320) + (x * cell).
                0 to: cell - 1 do: [ :dy |
                    0 to: cell - 1 do: [ :dx |
                        idx := base + (dy * 320) + dx + 1.
                        (dx = (cell - 1) or: [ dy = (cell - 1) ])
                            ifTrue: [ buf at: idx put: gridC ]
                            ifFalse: [ buf at: idx put: colour ] ] ] ] ].
        pane blit: buf.
        ^self
    ]

    drawHud [
        "The text overlay is RETAINED between frames, so it has to be cleared
         before redrawing or each generation's digits print over the last
         until the whole line smears solid."
        | state |
        pane textClear.
        state := running ifTrue: [ 'RUNNING' ] ifFalse: [ 'PAUSED' ].
        pane
            text: 'GEN ', gen printString, '   POP ', self population printString,
                  '   ', state
            x: 4 y: 4 r: 235 g: 240 b: 255 scale: 1.
        "Keep this line SHORT: the 5x7 font at scale 1 steps 6 pixels a
         character, so 320 pixels is 53 characters and the pane clips silently
         past that — the first draft lost its final 'IT' off the right edge."
        pane
            text: 'SPACE pause   LEFT draw   RIGHT erase   ESC quit'
            x: 4 y: 228 r: 150 g: 165 b: 200 scale: 1.
        ^self
    ]
]
